From 6526b472a807b4a86a16991260aca0438e18e23e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 22:46:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08077/11362] [outer-loop] Switch the live ARC generator to
 QAT, on VRAM not on quality

Operator directive: "switch to QAT to save the 1GB of VRAM."

unsloth/gemma-4-31B-it-GGUF Q4_K_M -> unsloth/gemma-4-31B-it-qat-GGUF
UD-Q4_K_XL. Measured live: 21296 MiB resident WITH the QAT drafter vs 21418
MiB for non-QAT WITHOUT one, so MTP speculation now costs less than nothing
against the prior baseline.

The switch is NOT justified on quality, and a test enforces that reading. A
13-game head-to-head had QAT ahead 5-2; the pre-registered extension to 20
games INVERTED it to 6-6, sign test p = 1.0 over 12 discordant pairs. The
13-game number is the one someone would find first, so
test_switch_is_not_justified_on_quality pins the constants block to say
INDISTINGUISHABLE out loud.

The hazard this could have shipped: both repos name their drafter
mtp-gemma-4-31B-it-Q8_0.gguf. The filename cannot tell them apart, and
_resolve_mtp_head globbed every models--*GGUF root and took sorted(...)[0] --
where the non-QAT repo sorts FIRST. With the old head still on this box, a QAT
target would have been served by a non-QAT drafter: accepted by llama.cpp,
forbidden by Google's model card, and invisible except as degraded draft
acceptance. _resolve_mtp_head is now repo-scoped, with a test that reproduces
both heads on disk and fails if the scoping is removed.

Also here, from the same session:

- Change-sparsity sweep over the roster (prompted by Mage-VL, arXiv
  2607.24904). wa30's 0.81% changed-cells did NOT generalise: the roster runs
  0.89%-26.6%, and vc33 (1.66x) and ft09 (1.40x) are WORSE than full grids
  under a changed-cell encoding. My earlier 10x extrapolation from wa30 alone
  was too optimistic and is retracted by this measurement.

TWO RECORD-INTEGRITY REPAIRS, both defects I introduced or surfaced this
session, recorded rather than quietly fixed:

1. The acknowledgement-writer REPLACED provenance.freshness_acknowledgements
   instead of appending, collapsing a 9-link chain to 3 and destroying 7
   committed arc_executable_world_model.py drift records in each of three
   artifacts. No path lost coverage, which is why a path-level check saw
   nothing -- but each destroyed link recorded a distinct drift event with its
   own sha256_was -> sha256_now. All 30 committed links restored verbatim,
   new ones appended, verified zero lost.

2. exp6011/6012/6013 were rebuilt (preferred over acknowledgement where a
   rebuild is possible), and each rebuild silently DROPPED the committed
   original_timings_superseded_by_rebuild block, because the scripts rewrite
   the artifact wholesale rather than merging. Restored by hand. This is the
   same never-prune-by-machine class as the 2026-07-29 test-suite incident.

The rebuilds themselves are clean: 0 substantive values moved across all
three. The 42/77/37 changed values are per-row elapsed_s, duration_s,
measurement_wall_s, the two provenance shas the lint tracks, and exp6012's
reproducibility_checksum -- which is a sha256 of its own payload including its
own timings, so it cannot hold still across a rerun. Every honest_verdict,
acceptance gate and population count is byte-identical.

